.TH EXIT 2
.SH NAME
exit \- terminate process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B _Noreturn void exit(int status);
.PP
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B _Noreturn void _exit(int status);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Exit
is the normal means of terminating a process.
.I Exit
closes all the process's files and notifies the parent process
if it is executing a
.IR wait .
The low-order 8 bits of 
.I status
are available to the parent process.
.PP
This call can never return.
.PP
The C function
.I exit
may cause cleanup actions before the
final `sys exit'.
The function
.I _exit
circumvents all cleanup.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
wait(2), fclose(3), atexit(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The cleanup is reached through a pointer, never by name
.I Crt0
tail-jumps to
.IR exit ,
so
.IR exit 's
object is linked into
.I every
program.  Naming the stdio cleanup outright \- which is what v7 does \- would drag
in the whole of stdio behind it: the stream table, the two buffers,
.IR malloc ,
.I free
and
.IR close ,
whether the program ever printed or not.
.B The `hello' program measured 2255 words that way and 100 through the pointer.
.PP
.B A weak definition cannot substitute, and that is a property of linkers.
.I B6ld
pulls an archive member only for a symbol that is still
.I undefined,
so a weak no-op would satisfy the reference and stop the real cleanup from ever
being pulled \- the opposite of what is wanted.
.IR Fclose (3)'s
.IR _flsbuf ()
arms the pointer instead, on the first buffered write, which every write path
passes through.
.PP
.B There are two such pointers now, not one.
.IR Atexit (3)
arms the second, and it is a second pointer rather than a general mechanism the
stdio flush was folded into, for exactly the reason above: the handler table is
32 words, and every program would carry them.
.I Exit
runs the registered handlers first and flushes afterwards, which is the order
C11 sec 7.22.4.4 requires \- a handler is allowed to print.
.SS The two live in different files, and the names could not collide
The library builds into a flat object directory, so
.IR exit 's
source is called
.I cuexit.c
\- v7's own name for it \- because
.I exit.c
would collide with the
.I _exit
stub's
.IR exit.S .
.SS _exit's stub has no return path
A call that never comes back has nothing to test r14 for and no return
instruction, which is why it is hand-written rather than generated.  The same is
true of
.IR exec (2).
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_exit
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
